          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Prove that any two nonabelian groups of order 21 are isomorphic.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"abstract_algebra\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidates\": [{\"candidate_id\": \"choice-1\", \"text\": \"Let G and H be arbitrary nonabelian groups of order 21. By Sylow’s theorems, each has a unique subgroup of order 7, since the number of Sylow 7-subgroups divides 3 and is congruent to 1 modulo 7.\\n\\nChoose a∈G of order 3 and b∈G of order 7. Then B=⟨b⟩ is normal. Also ⟨a⟩∩B={e}, so the 21 elements b^s a^r (0≤s<7, 0≤r<3) are distinct and hence comprise G. Normality gives\\n\\na b a⁻¹=b^k\\n\\nfor some k∈(Z/7Z)×. Since a³=e, conjugating b three times gives b=b^{k³}, so k³≡1 (mod 7). The roots are 1,2,4. If k=1, then a and b commute, making G=⟨a,b⟩ abelian. Thus k∈{2,4}.\\n\\nSimilarly, choose c∈H of order 3 and d∈H of order 7. Then every element of H has a unique form d^s c^r, and\\n\\nc d c⁻¹=d^ℓ\\n\\nfor some ℓ∈{2,4}. Replacing c by c⁻¹ replaces ℓ by ℓ⁻¹ modulo 7; since 2⁻¹≡4 and 4⁻¹≡2 modulo 7, we may choose a generator c′ of ⟨c⟩ such that\\n\\nc′d(c′)⁻¹=d^k.\\n\\nDefine φ:G→H on the unique normal forms by\\n\\nφ(b^s a^r)=d^s(c′)^r.\\n\\nFor r,u modulo 3 and s,t modulo 7,\\n\\n(b^s a^r)(b^t a^u)=b^{s+k^r t}a^{r+u},\\n\\nand the identical formula holds in H with b,a replaced by d,c′. Therefore φ is a homomorphism. Uniqueness of the normal forms shows that φ is bijective. Hence G≅H. Since G and H were arbitrary, any two nonabelian groups of order 21 are isomorphic.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-2\", \"text\": \"Let G be a nonabelian group of order 21. By Sylow’s theorems, the number n_7 of Sylow 7-subgroups satisfies n_7 ≡ 1 (mod 7) and n_7 | 3, so n_7=1. Thus the Sylow 7-subgroup P=⟨b⟩ is normal. By Cauchy’s theorem, G also contains an element a of order 3.\\n\\nBecause P is normal, conjugation by a induces an automorphism of P, so\\n\\na b a^{-1}=b^r\\n\\nfor some r∈(Z/7Z)^×. Since a^3=e, conjugating three times gives b=b^{r^3}, hence r^3≡1 (mod 7). The solutions are r≡1,2,4 (mod 7). If r=1, then a and b commute. Moreover, ⟨a⟩∩⟨b⟩={e}, so |⟨a⟩⟨b⟩|=3·7=21; hence a and b generate G, which would make G abelian. Therefore r is 2 or 4. Replacing a by a^{-1} replaces r by r^{-1}; since 4^{-1}≡2 (mod 7), we may choose a so that\\n\\na b a^{-1}=b^2.\\n\\nNow let G and H be arbitrary nonabelian groups of order 21. By the preceding argument, choose generators a,b∈G and c,d∈H such that\\n\\na^3=c^3=e,  b^7=d^7=e,  a b a^{-1}=b^2,  c d c^{-1}=d^2.\\n\\nEvery element of G has a unique expression a^i b^j with 0≤i<3 and 0≤j<7: existence follows from |⟨a⟩⟨b⟩|=21, and uniqueness follows from ⟨a⟩∩⟨b⟩={e}. The same holds in H. Define\\n\\nφ(a^i b^j)=c^i d^j.\\n\\nThis is therefore a well-defined bijection. From a b a^{-1}=b^2, we obtain\\n\\nb^j a^k=a^k b^{j2^{-k}},\\n\\nwhere exponents are taken modulo 7. Consequently,\\n\\n(a^i b^j)(a^k b^ℓ)=a^{i+k}b^{j2^{-k}+ℓ}.\\n\\nThe identical multiplication formula holds for c^i d^j in H. Hence φ preserves multiplication and is a homomorphism. Since it is bijective, φ is an isomorphism. Thus any two nonabelian groups of order 21 are isomorphic.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"Let \\\\(G\\\\) and \\\\(H\\\\) be nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\). By Sylow’s theorems, the number of Sylow \\\\(7\\\\)-subgroups divides \\\\(3\\\\) and is congruent to \\\\(1\\\\pmod 7\\\\), so each group has a unique, hence normal, Sylow \\\\(7\\\\)-subgroup.\\n\\nFor \\\\(G\\\\), choose generators \\\\(b\\\\) of a Sylow \\\\(7\\\\)-subgroup and \\\\(a\\\\) of a Sylow \\\\(3\\\\)-subgroup. Their intersection is trivial, so \\\\(G=\\\\langle b\\\\rangle\\\\langle a\\\\rangle\\\\), and every element has a unique expression \\\\(b^s a^r\\\\), where \\\\(0\\\\le s<7\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\le r<3\\\\).\\n\\nSince \\\\(\\\\langle b\\\\rangle\\\\) is normal, conjugation by \\\\(a\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\naba^{-1}=b^i\\n\\\\]\\nfor some \\\\(i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,6\\\\}\\\\). Because \\\\(a^3=e\\\\), conjugating three times gives \\\\(b=b^{i^3}\\\\), hence \\\\(i^3\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod 7\\\\). The solutions are \\\\(i=1,2,4\\\\). If \\\\(i=1\\\\), then \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\) commute and generate \\\\(G\\\\), making \\\\(G\\\\) abelian, a contradiction. Thus \\\\(i=2\\\\) or \\\\(4\\\\). In the latter case, replacing \\\\(a\\\\) by \\\\(a^{-1}\\\\) changes the exponent to \\\\(4^{-1}\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod 7\\\\). Therefore we may choose \\\\(a,b\\\\) so that\\n\\\\[\\na^3=b^7=e,\\\\qquad aba^{-1}=b^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nApplying the same argument to \\\\(H\\\\), choose generators \\\\(c,d\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\nc^3=d^7=e,\\\\qquad cdc^{-1}=d^2,\\n\\\\]\\nand every element of \\\\(H\\\\) has a unique expression \\\\(d^s c^r\\\\).\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi:G\\\\to H,\\\\qquad \\\\phi(b^s a^r)=d^s c^r.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is well-defined by uniqueness of the normal forms. Since\\n\\\\[\\na^r b^t a^{-r}=b^{2^r t},\\n\\\\]\\nwe have\\n\\\\[\\n(b^s a^r)(b^t a^u)=b^{s+2^r t}a^{r+u}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe identical formula holds in \\\\(H\\\\), so \\\\(\\\\phi(xy)=\\\\phi(x)\\\\phi(y)\\\\). Hence \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is a homomorphism. It is bijective because it maps the unique \\\\(21\\\\) normal forms \\\\(b^s a^r\\\\) to the unique \\\\(21\\\\) normal forms \\\\(d^s c^r\\\\). Therefore \\\\(G\\\\cong H\\\\). Thus any two nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\) are isomorphic.\"}]}",
